{"title":"2001 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 11","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In a class of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/6/6/66644fd9f411299c3c1eabf151b5f601c9ef6c21.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$25$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> students, each student has at most one pet. Three-fifths of the students have cats, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/8/1183a12017e48198b3352789cc39b223b17945d6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20\\%$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"35\" height=\"16\" /> have dogs, three have elephants, and the other students have no pets. How many students have no pets?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/4/8d437437a97746c8aab919c319a6fe4f6f64dff3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{ (A) }\\ 5 \\qquad\\text{ (B) }\\ 4 \\qquad\\text{ (C) }\\ 3 \\qquad\\text{ (D) }\\ 2 \\qquad\\text{ (E) }\\ 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"388\" height=\"18\" />  \n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_11&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Because each student has at most one pet, we know that there cannot be any students with multiple pets. For example, a student in the class that owns a cat cannot own an elephant or a dog. We can also let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> be the number of students that don't own pets.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/c/4/ac40ce1434cda0943c408cc185f5976212d95801.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{5}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" /> of the students own cats, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/d/6/dd6d7c4a3b899dedd7fc1b96253e628776418689.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{3}{5} \\times 25 = 15$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"98\" height=\"38\" /> own cats.\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/8/1183a12017e48198b3352789cc39b223b17945d6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20\\%$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"35\" height=\"16\" /> of the students own dogs, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/6/4/c641daaa6648b6ad602ec229379c515de730d7d6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20\\% \\times 25 = \\frac{1}{5} \\times 25 = 5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"187\" height=\"38\" /> own dogs.\n</p><p>We can now set up an equation involving <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> and the other values:\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/d/c0d4a4f32878e062e877a00e37a507fe97675a42.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n + 15 + 5 + 3 = 25$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"158\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/8/848d4ccefc4d96482d45c9c60f8c5340cfb7f799.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n + 23 = 25$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"96\" height=\"15\" />\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/d/4cd139a52c67d4afe3e6d61f321a3b005f237571.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n = \\boxed {\\textbf {(D) } 2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"93\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~anabel.disher\n</p>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)\" title=\"2001 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8)\">2001 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8)</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems\" title=\"2001 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Answer_Key&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"2001 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Answer Key (page does not exist)\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://cemc.math.uwaterloo.ca/contests/past_contests.html\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_10\" title=\"2001 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 10\">Problem 10</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_12\" title=\"2001 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 12\">Problem 12</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td colspan=\"3\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"2001 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2001 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2001 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2001_CEMC_Gauss_(Grade_8)_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2001 CEMC Gauss (Grade 8) Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2